{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-148152",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Let $B$ be the event that a teenager started to play music before $6$ years old, $M$ be the event that a teenager is good at math, and ${T}_{1},{T}_{2},{T}_{3}$ be the events that a teenager plays piano, violin, or another instrument, respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({T}_{1})=0.3 &  & P(M|{T}_{1})=0.5 \\\\  & P({T}_{2})=0.2 &  & P(M|{T}_{2})=0.6 \\\\  & P({T}_{3})=0.5 &  & P(M|{T}_{3})=0.47\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${T}_{1}⊔{T}_{2}⊔{T}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({T}_{1})+P({T}_{2})+P({T}_{3})=0.3+0.2+0.5=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $10%$ of teenagers started to play before $6$ years old, we have\n\n$$\nP(B)=0.1.\n$$\n\n- Since $58%$ of students who started to play before $6$ years old are good at math, we have\n\n$$\nP(M|B)=0.58.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen teenager who is good at math started to play before $6$ years old, which is represented as $P(B|M).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(B|M) & =\\frac{P(M|B)P(B)}{P(M)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(M|B)P(B)}{P(M|{T}_{1})P({T}_{1})+P(M|{T}_{2})P({T}_{2})+P(M|{T}_{3})P({T}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.58)(0.1)}{(0.5)(0.3)+(0.6)(0.2)+(0.47)(0.5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.058}{0.505} \\\\  & ≈0.115,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.",
